---
title: SSH Basics
order: 1
description: ssh connection, ~/.ssh/config, options, escape sequences
tags: [ssh, connection, config, remote]
---

Core SSH usage for connecting to remote hosts, configuring persistent
settings, and controlling active sessions.

@code bash
# ---- basic connections ----

ssh user@hostname                         # connect
ssh user@hostname -p 2222                 # custom port
ssh hostname                              # uses current username
ssh user@192.168.1.10                     # by IP
ssh user@hostname command                 # run single command and exit
ssh user@hostname 'ls -la /var/log'       # remote command with args
ssh -t user@hostname htop                 # force TTY allocation (interactive)

@code bash
# ---- common options ----

ssh -v user@hostname                      # verbose (debug)
ssh -vv user@hostname                     # more verbose
ssh -vvv user@hostname                    # maximum verbosity
ssh -i ~/.ssh/mykey user@hostname         # specify identity file
ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no user@host # skip host key check (first connect)
ssh -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null user@host    # don't save host key
ssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 user@hostname     # connection timeout
ssh -o ServerAliveInterval=60 user@host   # keepalive every 60s
ssh -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 user@host    # disconnect after 3 missed keepalives
ssh -N user@hostname                      # no remote command (for tunnels)
ssh -f user@hostname -N                   # fork to background (for tunnels)
ssh -q user@hostname                      # quiet mode

@text
~/.ssh/config provides persistent per-host settings. This avoids typing
long option strings repeatedly.

@code bash
# ---- ~/.ssh/config ----

# global defaults
Host *
    ServerAliveInterval 60
    ServerAliveCountMax 3
    AddKeysToAgent yes
    IdentitiesOnly yes

# named host
Host myserver
    HostName 192.168.1.10
    User admin
    Port 2222
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_myserver

# wildcard
Host *.dev.example.com
    User developer
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_dev

# jump host (ProxyJump)
Host internal
    HostName 10.0.0.5
    User admin
    ProxyJump jumphost

Host jumphost
    HostName bastion.example.com
    User jump
    Port 2222

# connection reuse (multiplexing)
Host *
    ControlMaster auto
    ControlPath ~/.ssh/sockets/%r@%h:%p
    ControlPersist 10m

@text
With the config above, `ssh myserver` connects to 192.168.1.10:2222 as
admin using the specified key. Create the sockets directory:
`mkdir -p ~/.ssh/sockets`

@code bash
# ---- escape sequences (during active session) ----

# press Enter, then:
~.                                        # disconnect (kill hung session)
~^Z                                       # suspend session (fg to resume)
~#                                        # list forwarded connections
~&                                        # background session (on logout)
~?                                        # show escape help
~C                                        # open command line (add forwards on the fly)
~~                                        # send literal ~ character

# the ~C command line lets you add forwards mid-session:
# ssh> -L 8080:localhost:80               # add local forward
# ssh> -R 9090:localhost:80               # add remote forward
# ssh> -KL 8080                           # cancel local forward

@code bash
# ---- known hosts management ----

ssh-keygen -R hostname                    # remove host from known_hosts
ssh-keygen -F hostname                    # find host in known_hosts
ssh-keyscan hostname                      # fetch host public key
ssh-keyscan -H hostname >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts    # add hashed entry

@code bash
# ---- SSH on Artix (OpenRC) ----

# install
pacman -S openssh

# server (sshd)
rc-service sshd start
rc-update add sshd default                # start on boot

# config location
# /etc/ssh/sshd_config                    # server config
# /etc/ssh/ssh_config                     # system-wide client config
# ~/.ssh/config                           # per-user client config

@text
Security best practices:
- Disable root login: `PermitRootLogin no` in sshd_config
- Disable password auth: `PasswordAuthentication no` (use keys only)
- Use a non-standard port to reduce noise (not a security measure)
- Use `AllowUsers` or `AllowGroups` to restrict access
- Keep SSH updated: `pacman -Syu openssh`
